> > The other way is to remember Nethack is not real life, and has very non-real
> > physical laws. eg: The hypotenuse of a triangle is equal to the maximum
> > of the leg lengths and lichen will block your passage as well as a mastodon.

And of course, a single person can open/close a door large enough to
permit or block the passage of a mastodon in a single turn.

> The thing that always gets me is the way you're able to function just
> as well when you have *one* hit point as when you have a hundred.

Well, apparently the average character is also able to handle sleep
deprivation exceptionally well (at least i assume a game must sum up
to several days) - i'd expect them to be constantly hallucinating from
turn 20 000 onwards. And they also always seem to be moving at the
same speed, no matter whether they're floundering about in unexplored
territory while blind or running in panic from an angry leocrotta.

> Also, apart from a very few special attacks (i.e. poison, decapitation
> etc) you're never permanently handicapped by any fighting. In real
> life, sooner or later an enemy arrow would take out your eye and you'd
> be blinded for the rest of the game. Or you'd lose an arm or a leg.

Well, i _guess_ that this is to render the game somewhat playable. I
mean, once both your arms and legs are off, how are you supposed to
fight[1]?

> But in Nethack, somehow these essential body parts are magically
> regenerated (or invulnerable.) Not very logical...

Well, errm, i guess you've been equipped with medieval regeneration
nano-tech...

[1] yes, i know the canonical answer, but i mean, really? Actually,
once you've lost _one_ limb, you're not very likely to be able to
effectively fight again - ever.

